  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"93\" data-end=\"336\">Factor V (also called <strong data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"132\">Labile Factor</strong>) is a <strong data-start=\"139\" data-end=\"162\">coagulation protein</strong> involved in the <strong data-start=\"179\" data-end=\"197\">common pathway</strong> of the clotting cascade. It acts as a cofactor for the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin, which is essential for blood clot formation.</p>\n<p data-start=\"338\" data-end=\"438\">The <strong data-start=\"342\" data-end=\"368\">Factor V activity test</strong> measures the <strong data-start=\"382\" data-end=\"403\">functional amount</strong> of this clotting factor in plasma.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Factor V Activity (%)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>50&ndash;150%</strong></td>\n<td>Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>20&ndash;49%</strong></td>\n<td>Mild deficiency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5&ndash;20%</strong></td>\n<td>Moderate deficiency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&lt;5%</strong></td>\n<td>Severe deficiency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"876\" data-end=\"930\"><strong data-start=\"880\" data-end=\"930\">Low Factor V Levels &ndash; Causes &amp; Interpretation:</strong></p>\n<p data-start=\"932\" data-end=\"991\"><strong data-start=\"937\" data-end=\"991\">1. Congenital Factor V Deficiency (Parahemophilia)</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"992\" data-end=\"1242\">\n<li data-start=\"992\" data-end=\"1027\">\n<p data-start=\"994\" data-end=\"1027\">Rare autosomal recessive disorder</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1028\" data-end=\"1112\">\n<p data-start=\"1030\" data-end=\"1112\">Symptoms: Easy bruising, nosebleeds, menorrhagia, bleeding after surgery or trauma</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1113\" data-end=\"1149\">\n<p data-start=\"1115\" data-end=\"1149\">Severity depends on Factor V level</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1150\" data-end=\"1182\">\n<p data-start=\"1152\" data-end=\"1182\">PT and aPTT both <strong data-start=\"1169\" data-end=\"1182\">prolonged</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1183\" data-end=\"1242\">\n<p data-start=\"1185\" data-end=\"1242\">Mixing study corrects clotting time (rules out inhibitor)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1244\" data-end=\"1284\"><strong data-start=\"1249\" data-end=\"1284\">2. Acquired Factor V Deficiency</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"1285\" data-end=\"1552\">\n<li data-start=\"1285\" data-end=\"1339\">\n<p data-start=\"1287\" data-end=\"1339\"><strong data-start=\"1287\" data-end=\"1304\">Liver disease</strong> (Factor V is synthesized in liver)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1340\" data-end=\"1390\">\n<p data-start=\"1342\" data-end=\"1390\"><strong data-start=\"1342\" data-end=\"1390\">Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1391\" data-end=\"1442\">\n<p data-start=\"1393\" data-end=\"1442\"><strong data-start=\"1393\" data-end=\"1416\">Massive transfusion</strong> (dilutional coagulopathy)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1443\" data-end=\"1488\">\n<p data-start=\"1445\" data-end=\"1488\"><strong data-start=\"1445\" data-end=\"1463\">Autoantibodies</strong> to Factor V (inhibitors)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1489\" data-end=\"1552\">\n<p data-start=\"1491\" data-end=\"1552\"><strong data-start=\"1491\" data-end=\"1514\">Certain medications</strong> (e.g., antibiotics like beta-lactams)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1559\" data-end=\"1588\"><strong data-start=\"1563\" data-end=\"1588\">High Factor V Levels:</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"1589\" data-end=\"1794\">\n<li data-start=\"1589\" data-end=\"1639\">\n<p data-start=\"1591\" data-end=\"1639\">Usually <strong data-start=\"1599\" data-end=\"1629\">not clinically significant</strong> by itself</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1640\" data-end=\"1794\">\n<p data-start=\"1642\" data-end=\"1794\"><strong data-start=\"1642\" data-end=\"1670\">Factor V Leiden mutation</strong> is unrelated to elevated activity; instead, it's a mutation causing resistance to activated Protein C (measured separately)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
